A reverse vending machine takes your empty bottle and gives you a small refund. In Norway and Germany these machines push recycling rates above 90 percent. In Pakistan you almost never see one.

The reason is price. A commercial unit costs between $6,500 and $25,000. Most of that money goes into sensors: barcode scanners, infrared spectrometers, weight and metal detectors.

So for my final year project I asked a simple question. Can one camera and one cheap CPU do the same job?

## The build

The machine has a camera, IR sensors that stop the bottle in front of the lens, a C# touchscreen app, and an STM32F407 that drives the sorting servos. No GPU anywhere.

I trained YOLO11n on 4,560 photos I took inside the machine itself. On the held-out test set it reached **mAP@0.5 of 0.993**, with precision 0.970 and recall 0.992.

The whole system cost **$1,087**. That is 83 percent less than the cheapest comparable commercial unit.

## The surprise

Accuracy was not the hard part. Speed on a CPU was.

I ran three generations of YOLO on the same machine, on 2,247 real frames, and measured latency, CPU load and energy per detection.

| Model | Latency | Energy per detection |
|---|---|---|
| YOLO11n | 131.4 ms | 3,211.8 mJ |
| YOLO26 | about 40% slower | about 44% more energy |

The newest model lost. On a cheap CPU, newer architecture did not mean faster. It meant more work per frame.

> If your hardware is fixed, benchmark on that hardware. Leaderboards are measured on GPUs you will never ship.

## What I would do next

Test the same comparison on an ARM board, since that is what a real low-cost product would use.

This work is under review at IEEE ICASSP 2027.
